# Pseudonymized data only

Per `docs/requirements/project-requirements.md` Section 7: the system holds personal data of real
people. **Never put real personal data anywhere in this repo, in logs, or in agent/chat context.**
This is a hard rule, not a style preference — a leak here is treated the same as an access-control
leak.

Ported from `.claude/rules/pseudonymized-data-only.md` — keep both in sync by hand if either
changes.

## What counts as real personal data

Names, personal emails/phones, residential addresses, birthdays, photos, emergency contacts, or
any other identifying detail belonging to an actual person — employee, PeopleForce candidate, or
timetracker user. Includes data pasted from Teams calls, Jira tickets, spreadsheets, or
screenshots, not just data typed directly into code.

## What to use instead

Pseudonymised data: **real structure and volume, substituted names and contacts.** Seed data,
fixtures, and test cases should match production shape (500+ employees, realistic org depth,
realistic project fan-out) with every identifying field fabricated.

## Where this applies

- **Committed files**: seed scripts, fixtures, test data, ADRs, integration research notes,
  meeting-note summaries in `docs/decisions/`.
- **Logs**: scrub real request payloads before logging, not just before committing.
- **Chat/agent context**: do not paste real employee/candidate records into a Cursor chat, even
  transiently. When debugging against real integration data (PeopleForce, timetracker), summarize
  the *shape* of the response (field names, types, pagination) rather than pasting real records.
- **Screenshots and exports**: any screenshot or `.xlsx` export attached to a PR or ticket must use
  pseudonymised data.

## Integration research

When recording PeopleForce/timetracker findings in `docs/integrations/`, capture endpoint shapes,
auth flows, rate limits, and field lists — not real candidate or employee records returned during
exploration.
